// The module semantics a package's test-layer program (`tsconfig.test.json`)
// shares. A test config extends its package's own build config FIRST and this
// file SECOND — `"extends": ["./tsconfig.json", "../../tsconfig.test.base.json"]`
// — so it inherits everything the build config decides (strictness, `types`,
// `paths`, `rootDir`) and this file overrides module semantics only.
//
// Why these values: vitest runs the tests as ESM through esbuild/vite, which
// does not require the explicit `.js` extensions NodeNext demands on relative
// imports. Matching vitest's resolver is fidelity, not laxity. `lib` is ES2022
// for the same reason (`Array.prototype.at` and friends). A test config that
// also needs `DOM` or `types` re-declares that one key itself.
//
// ⛔ No strictness option belongs here. If a test does not compile, that is
// the finding.
{
  "compilerOptions": {
    "noEmit": true,
    "module": "esnext",
    "moduleResolution": "bundler",
    "lib": ["ES2022"]
  }
}
